{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>וְהָיָה ה׳ לִי לֵאלֹהִים.</b> He accepted upon himself in his vow to serve HaShem from love and not from fear, for one who serves from fear is called עוֹבֵד אֱלֹהִים — that is, serving the attribute of strict justice, from which he is afraid — and one who serves from love is called עוֹבֵד ה׳, for love and mercy are one matter. Therefore it says ״וְהָיָה ה׳ לִי לֵאלֹהִים״. And if you wish to explain that the beginning of the vow is from ״וְהָאֶבֶן הַזֹּאת״ according to Rashi's explanation, then he said ״וְהָיָה ה׳ לִי״ — that He not judge me with the attribute of strict justice alone, but that He also join the name of mercy to the name אֱלֹהִים. This is what he said: ״וְהָיָה ה׳ לִי לֵאלֹהִים״ — He will join it to the name אֱלֹהִים; then ״וְהָאֶבֶן הַזֹּאת״ and so forth." "And in Bereshit Rabbah (70:4) they expounded ״אִם יִהְיֶה אֱלֹהִים עִמָּדִי וּשְׁמָרַנִי״ — that is, from four categories of transgressions, and they are: idolatry, forbidden sexual relations, bloodshed, and evil speech — and they supported it from the verse. It appears that this midrash as well wished to resolve this difficulty: was Jacob really in doubt about the promise of HaShem, blessed be He? Rather, because HaShem, blessed be He, had said to him ״כִּי לֹא אֶעֱזָבְךָ עַד אֲשֶׁר אִם עָשִׂיתִי״ (Genesis 28:15), the expression ״אִם״ intimated as though He had said 'whether I will do or whether I will not do' — and this depends on the matter, if sin does not cause it. Therefore he said ״אִם יִהְיֶה אֱלֹהִים עִמָּדִי וּשְׁמָרַנִי״ — from sin, and he specified these four categories of transgression, for they were the cause of the destruction of the first Temple and the second. Therefore he said that if HaShem guards him from these four categories of transgression, then ״וְהָאֶבֶן הַזֹּאת אֲשֶׁר שַׂמְתִּי מַצֵּבָה יִהְיֶה בֵּית אֱלֹהִים״ (Genesis 28:22). And from the affirmative one may infer the negative, for if they are not guarded from these four categories of transgression, then it will not be a house of Elohim, for it will be destroyed on their account."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {1 "The midrash citation reads ״ע ד״, likely a printer's abbreviation for chapter 70, section 4 of Bereshit Rabbah; resolved accordingly."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:30:57.484174949Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}